#![TODO]
// Regression test: WIR pipeline emits invalid core Wasm for a `match`
// arm whose body's last statement is a non-unit function call whose
// result is discarded by a trailing `;`.
//
// Validation error: type mismatch: expected i32 but nothing on stack
//
// Minimal trigger:
//   1. A `match` on an enum (or payload-bearing variant) with multiple
//      arms — at least one arm that follows the failing arm shape and
//      one arm typed as unit (e.g. `_ => {}`).
//   2. The failing arm's block body ends with `helper();` (trailing
//      `;`), where `helper()` returns a non-unit value.
//
// Replacing the call site with `let _ = helper();` avoids the trap
// (same workaround as `match_arm_struct_field_invalid_wasm.wado`),
// because `let _ = ...` lowers to `TirStmtKind::LetDestructure {
// pattern: Wildcard, ... }`, which `block_result_type` ignores. The
// WIR builder's drop-insertion then runs against the rewritten
// `TirStmtKind::Expr(value)` and the arms agree on `Unit`.
//
// Root cause (same family as `match_arm_struct_field_invalid_wasm.wado`):
//   * `parser::parse_expr_stmt_in_block` consumes the trailing `;`
//     but does NOT record its presence on the `ExprStmt` it
//     constructs — `Stmt::Expr(expr_stmt)` is indistinguishable from
//     a trailing-position expression (no `;`, must be followed by
//     `}`).
//   * `resolver::resolve_block` then emits the last statement as
//     `TirStmtKind::Expr(...)` regardless of whether the source had
//     a `;`, so `tir::block_result_type` reads it as the trailing
//     expression and reports the block's result as `i32`. The
//     `_ => {}` arm reports unit. The arms disagree, and the WIR
//     generator emits a `(if (result i32) ...)` whose `_` arm leaves
//     nothing on the stack.
//
// Fix (tracked separately): teach the parser to record whether the
// `;` was consumed (e.g. a `pub terminated: bool` field on
// `ExprStmt`) and have `block_result_type` return `Unit` when the
// last statement is an `Expr` whose source ended with `;`. Once
// that lands this fixture starts passing and the test runner will
// flag the `#![TODO]` for removal — at which point the comment
// header can be trimmed to just the workaround note.

enum Foo {
    A,
    B,
}

fn helper() -> i32 {
    return 42;
}

export fn run() {
    let x = Foo::A;
    match x {
        A => {
            helper();
        },
        _ => {},
    }
}

__DATA__
{
    "stdout": ""
}
